I’ll inspect the measurement metadata, pattern, and the frozen PXRD workflow guidance first. Then I’ll identify candidate phases, build/refine a multiphase GSAS-II model, and validate that every submitted artifact is internally consistent with the final project.

The chemistry narrows the search to Sr–W–O with possible SrCO₃/WO₃ remnants, but I’m treating that only as a prior. I’m now checking the frozen COD index and the project/replay format so candidate selection and the exported profile are scored from the same GSAS-II state.

The dominant pattern is unambiguously scheelite SrWO₄: its 101/112/200/204/220/116/312 sequence tracks the observed 18.33/27.99/33.39/45.47/47.75/51.98/56.08° peaks. Several strong peaks remain, so I’m separating their peak families before committing to the phase count; the low-angle 23–25° triplet is particularly diagnostic of residual monoclinic WO₃.

A two-phase model (scheelite SrWO₄ + monoclinic WO₃) explains most of the scan, including the full 23–25° WO₃ triplet and its higher-angle satellites. A coherent residual family remains near 29.75, 30.91, 32.60, and 44.65°, so I’m testing off-stoichiometric/analogue-derived strontium tungstates as well as residual Sr-bearing precursors before refinement.

The screening refinements are converging toward a three-phase explanation. SrCO₃ (both polymorphs), SrO, and rhombohedral Sr₃W₂O₉ refine to essentially zero abundance, while the off-stoichiometric tungsten-bronze analogue remains under active comparison.

The dominant phase is clearly scheelite SrWO₄, with a smaller monoclinic WO₃ contribution. I’m now testing whether the remaining peaks support a genuine third phase or are better explained by specimen/profile effects, then I’ll freeze and replay the final GSAS-II model for internally consistent CIFs, fractions, and profile output.

The residual peak set has resolved into a coherent third-phase fingerprint: a tetragonal tungsten-bronze-type Sr₄.₂₆W₁₀O₃₀ analogue. Its independent lines at about 29.75°, 30.89°, 32.63°, 35.32°, 53.44°, and 67.0° all track the data; the required lattice change from the Ca prototype is anisotropic and chemically sensible for Sr substitution. I’m refining that adapted structure now rather than treating those peaks as miscellaneous residuals.

The third phase is not the bronze candidate after all: refinement strongly supports a Sr₃WO₆ ordered-perovskite analogue derived from Ba₃WO₆. Adding it drops Rwp from 15.96% to 14.63% and gives ~11.5 wt%; importantly, SrWO₄ + WO₃ + Sr₃WO₆ then closes the starting 1:1 Sr:W balance to about 0.97. I’m now refining specimen and phase-broadening terms while enforcing physical bounds—the first unconstrained pass exposed a correlated, slightly negative roughness coefficient that will not be retained.

The residuals split into two reproducible families. A fergusonite-type SrWO₄ polymorph accounts for the 29.75/48.57/59.6° family, while the adapted bronze cell accounts for 22.09/30.90/32.6/44.67/53.44/67.0°. A five-phase trial is materially better (Rwp 12.67% before broadening/texture refinement); I’m checking whether both minority phases remain stable once those nuisance terms are introduced, which is the key guard against over-identification.

The nuisance-term check confirms real surface roughness: forcing a flat specimen worsens Rwp from 12.55% to about 14.54% even before size refinement. The bounded profile runs are still converging; I’m also auditing the limit handling because GSAS-II did not freeze an exploratory coefficient at the requested boundary on its first cycle.

The five-phase model is still the strongest chemically coherent solution. I’m finishing a constrained refinement now, then I’ll replay the saved GSAS-II project to generate the exact submitted profile and cross-check every CIF and fraction against it.

The constrained run converged to a physically valid profile (Rwp ≈ 12.1%) and all five phase scales remain nonzero. Several broadening terms hit allowed bounds, so I’m treating that as a warning—not as extra certainty—and am now separating structure/intensity corrections from peak-width effects before freezing the project.

The roughness-free control is decisively worse (Rwp 14.8% versus about 12.2%), so a low-angle surface-roughness correction is supported. Texture testing on the dominant scheelite phase changes Rwp by only 0.01 percentage point and returns a ratio near 1, so I’m not retaining that unnecessary parameter; the minority phases are being checked the same way.

Two minority phases show clear, axis-specific texture signatures: the Sr₃WO₆-like phase favors [100], and fergusonite favors [111], each at the permitted edge of the March–Dollase range. I’ll retain them provisionally, but the boundary values will be called out as correlated with the analogue-derived structures unless coordinate refinement relaxes them.

The combined texture model is now more selective: Sr₃WO₆ [100] plus bronze [100] lowers Rwp to 11.67%, while adding the apparent fergusonite or WO₃ texture actually worsens the joint fit. I’m discarding those two effects and moving to shared per-phase displacement factors and small symmetry-preserving coordinate adjustments.

A coordinate-bound convention check caught a GSAS-II detail: those variables store absolute fractional coordinates, not coordinate deltas. I discarded the affected trial projects before use and corrected the limits around each starting site; no submitted structure is based on those invalid trials.

The bounded-coordinate replay is stable and physically screened. Small shifts improve scheelite, WO₃, and fergusonite; the bronze coordinate gain vanishes once displacements are kept near the generator’s stated mismatch scale, so I’m leaving the bronze parent coordinates intact rather than overfitting that weak phase.

The accepted small-coordinate model now replays at Rwp 11.45% with no physical review flags. The shifts remain within a conservative neighborhood of the references; adding bronze-coordinate freedom worsens replay, so that phase stays at the analogue geometry. I’m doing one final pass on peak broadening and then will lock and export the project.

The broadening check finds one defensible anisotropic term: fergusonite strain along [010] differs from the transverse value while staying inside the laboratory’s allowed anisotropy ratio. Other apparent gains require boundary-violating ratios or change replay negligibly, so I’m excluding them.

The analogue phases benefit from only very small heavy-atom adjustments: about 0.04 Å in Sr₃WO₆ and 0.05–0.07 Å on the bronze W/Sr sites. Those shifts are comparable to the stated reference-mismatch scale and lower replay Rwp to 11.09%; I’m keeping them while continuing to reject larger coordinate excursions.

I also enforced the framework’s hard roughness-generation cap (no more than a 50% intensity deficit at 20°). The unconstrained fit preferred a stronger deficit, but that realization is impossible for this benchmark; the best allowed correction is being used even though it raises observed-data Rwp by about 0.16 percentage point.

The final physically constrained model is now locked: five phases, normalized fractions stable to within roughly one percentage point across the accepted refinements, and native replay Rwp = 11.12% with no GSAS-II physical-review flags. I’m exporting directly from that replay so the CIFs, fractions, and submitted curve are byte-for-model consistent with `final.gpx`.

A final CIF audit caught one special-position rounding/refinement issue in the bronze model: the Sr site had drifted off its `y = x + 1/2` constraint, which duplicated the site on symmetry expansion. I’m enforcing that exact relation in the project and replaying again; the Sr₃WO₆ model’s sub-0.8 Å contacts are instead genuine half-occupied split sites and will be explicitly marked as a structural uncertainty.

The export set is complete. I’m doing one last consistency pass now: JSON/schema, phase-fraction normalization, CIF readability, exact profile alignment, and confirmation that `final.gpx` reproduces the submitted model.

The first file comparison used the submitted descriptive filenames against the replay’s numbered export names, so those “DIFF” labels were only a naming mismatch. I’m mapping the five replay exports by composition and checking their numerical identity and the profile arrays directly.

All five CIFs parse cleanly with normalized fractions and no invalid models. Two explicitly documented geometry-review items remain (WO₃ close O sites and the Sr₃WO₆ half-occupied split sites); neither is a hard physical failure. I’m now checking exact CIF↔GPX phase correspondence and the submitted fit through the evaluator’s own validation routines.

Analysis complete. Fractions are normalized over the modeled crystalline phases:

| Phase | Weight fraction |
|---|---:|
| Scheelite SrWO₄, I4₁/a | 60.60% |
| Monoclinic WO₃, P2₁/n | 10.69% |
| Sr₃WO₆-like split-site phase, P1 | 8.32% |
| Fergusonite SrWO₄, I2/a | 9.20% |
| Sr₄.₂₆W₁₀O₃₀ tetragonal bronze, P4/mbm | 11.19% |

The verified native GSAS-II replay gives Rwp = 11.126%, Rp = 7.992%, Rexp = 3.527%, and GOF = 3.155 across all 8,282 observations. The CIFs correspond one-to-one with the phases in `final.gpx`, and their fractions agree to numerical precision.

